> You're building an IPL scorecard                                 (HashSet → no duplicates, elements are always in sorted (natural or custom) order , 
    > Batsmen are added to the leaderboard in random order          (Backed by a Red-Black tree → all operations are O(log n))
        > but the display should be always sorted by runs.

import java.util.TreeSet;

public class IplLeaderboard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // TreeSet<Integer> keeps the runs automatically sorted
        TreeSet<Integer> topScores = new TreeSet<>();

        // Batsmen score these runs in the tournament (insertion order is messy)
        topScores.add(87);
        topScores.add(42);
        topScores.add(103);
        topScores.add(42);    // duplicate — silently ignored
        topScores.add(65);
        topScores.add(99);
        topScores.add(103);   // duplicate — silently ignored

        System.out.println("All scores (auto-sorted): " + topScores);

        // Rank them — highest first
        System.out.println("\nTop 3 run-scorers (high to low):");
        int rank = 1;
        for (int score : topScores.descendingSet()) {
            if (rank > 3) break;
            System.out.println("  " + rank++ + ". " + score + " runs");
        }

        // Quick queries TreeSet is great at
        System.out.println("\nLowest score:  " + topScores.first());
        System.out.println("Highest score: " + topScores.last());
    }
}


o/p-------------------------------
All scores (auto-sorted): [42, 65, 87, 99, 103]

Top 3 run-scorers (high to low):
  1. 103 runs
  2. 99 runs
  3. 87 runs

Lowest score:  42
Highest score: 103

----------------------------------------

// Why TreeSet? A HashSet would give random order, a LinkedHashSet would give insertion order. 
// Only TreeSet guarantees sorted order — perfect for a leaderboard. And first() / last() / descendingSet() are built in for free.
